    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHICH HALF OF CHANDRA-MERLIN THIS USES, AND WHY THAT MATTERS. Only the SOUND half -- a homomorphism implies containment -- is load-bearing for the three positive answers. The converse (containment implies a homomorphism) is the deeper direction and, as AHV Exercise 6.12a notes, it needs the underlying domain to be INFINITE. This fact never invokes it: the three negative answers are not 'no homomorphism was found, therefore no containment', they are 'here is a concrete finite database on which the two queries return different answer sets', established by enumerating every assignment. So the infinite-domain hypothesis is not in the footprint, because the argument that needs it is not in the proof.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE BEST SHOWCASE IN THE LEDGER FOR THE PROJECT'S OWN SENTENCE. Finding the homomorphism is NP-complete. Checking it is a nested loop over the atoms of one query. `untrusted fast search, trusted small checking` is not an analogy here, it is the literal shape of the artifact: the certificate for `Q_terse is contained in Q_verbose` is four variable-to-element pairs.",
